            <listPerson>
                <person xml:id="MAT05" sex="1">
                    <persName>
                        <roleName/>
                        <forename>William</forename>
                        <surname>Mathews</surname>
                        <addName/>
                    </persName>
                    <birth when="1769">
                        <placeName/>
                    </birth>
                    <death when="1801"/>
                    <occupation>Collaborated with Wordsworth on The Philanthropist</occupation>
                    <note type="editorial">
                        <p> The entry for 15 August 1795 has ‘Wordsworth and Matthews call, nah’.
                            This ‘Matthews’ was most likely Wordsworth’s acquaintance William Mathews (one ‘t’) (1769-1801), the son of a London
                            book-seller and medicine vendor and brother of Charles Mathews
                            (1776-1835) the famous comic actor. Acquainted with Wordsworth at
                            Cambridge, they collaborated on a projected periodical The
                            Philanthropist in 1794. 
                            We have only coded those 'Matthews' as William when there is a clear link to Wordsworth. Further details, Letters of William and
                            Dorothy Wordsworth, ed. E. de Selincourt, 2nd edn, The Early Years,
                            1787-1805, rev. C. L. Shaver (Oxford, 1967), 48 fn.  </p>
                    </note>
